---
import TimerPage from '@/layouts/TimerPage.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import { faqs } from '@/data/faqs';
import { relatedTimers, relatedPosts } from '@/data/related';
import { defaultConfigs } from '@/engine/presets';
import { meditations } from '@/data/meditations';
---

<TimerPage
  title="Meditation Timer – Choose Your Session and Interval Bells"
  description="Choose a meditation session from 1 to 180 minutes, with optional start, interval and end bells. Keep one session clock and adjust the bells to suit you."
  path="/meditation"
  h1="Meditation timer with interval bells"
  lede="This meditation timer keeps one clock for your whole sit. A soft bell can ring every 10 minutes, and one rings at the end. Nothing else makes a sound."
  config={defaultConfigs.meditation}
  faq={faqs['/meditation']}
  related={relatedTimers['/meditation']!}
  posts={relatedPosts['/meditation']}
  crumbName="Meditation timer"
>
  <Section title="What is meditation?" flush>
    <p>
      Meditation includes several practices for working with attention. In a simple breath-focused
      practice, notice your natural breathing and gently return when your attention wanders. You can
      reserve a quiet moment after breakfast, during a break or after work; choose a time when you
      can safely give it your attention.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our <a href="/blog/how-long-to-meditate">introduction to meditation</a> explains a first short
      practice, possible benefits and what the evidence can tell you. An end bell simply marks the
      space you have reserved.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Choose what each bell means">
    <p>
      For a quiet ten-minute sit, set Session length to 10, turn Interval bell off, leave Start bell
      off and keep End bell on. Use Preview bell to check the tone and device volume before settling
      in.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a session with two parts, set twenty minutes and a bell every ten. You will hear one
      intermediate bell and one end bell. Decide whether the midpoint marks a change of practice or
      a simple check-in. The <a href="/blog/meditation-timer-interval-bells">interval-bell guide</a>{' '}
      has more examples.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Understand the whole-session clock">
    <p>
      The default is thirty minutes with bells at ten and twenty minutes, then one end bell at
      thirty. The large countdown measures the whole session; it does not restart at each bell.
      Start, interval and end bells have separate switches.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the interval does not divide the session evenly, the final part is shorter. A 25-minute
      session with ten-minute bells has cues at ten and twenty, then ends at twenty-five. An
      interval at the exact ending is not played twice.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Choose a session length">
    <p>
      Pick a length that fits your practice and available time. Longer is not automatically better.
      Read <a href="/blog/how-long-to-meditate">how to choose a meditation length</a>, or open one
      of these schedules.
    </p>
    <ul class="links">
      {meditations.map((m) => (
        <li>
          <a href={m.path}>{m.h1}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Screen and sound">
    <p>
      Keep the page visible when you need the bell. The timer requests an awake screen, but device
      settings can refuse it. A locked phone or background page may miss sound cues even though
      elapsed time is correct when the page resumes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sound off mutes all selected bells. Vibration may still occur on devices that support it. For
      offline use, first load this page online and test a reload without a connection. For distinct
      work and rest phases, choose the <a href="/interval">interval timer</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>
</TimerPage>
